# Projects and tasks

- **Which project a run belongs to** is settled once, when it is created (`ProjectScopeService.placeNewRun`), and stored as `Run.projectId` + `Run.projectSource` (`explicit` / `card` / `folder` / `none`): the project the run was started under by choice (`projectId` on the chat and team-run create DTOs, from a run configuration or the composer chip; an unknown or deleted id is passed over), explicitly none (`projectId: null`), else its task card's project while it exists, else the live project bound to its folder or to the CLOSEST folder above it — after `ProjectRootsService.rootsOf` maps a task worktree to its repository (`ProjectDao.findNearestByFolder` over `nearestProjectOf`). The home folder and `/` count only as an exact match, never as an ancestor. A run whose project could not be read keeps `projectSource` null and is resolved again where asked (`projectOfRun`: card, then exact folder — the rule every run from before the columns keeps). The run's instructions snapshot, its memory index and its memory scope all follow the one answer. A run started under a project by choice, with no group named, is filed in that project's sidebar group while it exists (`RunGroupsService.groupForNewRun`). The run wire names it (`RunWire.project`, from one live-projects read per listing; null for none, a deleted one and a run from before the columns).
- `GET /v1/projects/of-folder?path=` answers the project a new chat there would get by the folder rule (null for a folder that does not exist).
- A project's instructions are blocks (`Project.instructionBlocks`, JSON `{id, title, text}[]`, every block reaching every run). `Project.instructions` is derived on every write: the blocks' texts joined in order, titles left out, and it is what a run's snapshot composes. A project written before blocks has `instructionBlocks` null and reads as one block titled Instructions (`projectBlocksOf`); its first write stores blocks. The change log and an analysis card's diff use `blocksDocument` (each block under `## Title`), so a change names its block.
- `Project.instructionsRevision` counts every change to the description or instructions (an edit that leaves `blocksDocument` as it was, such as a blank block added, moves nothing). `ProjectsService.update` is their one writer: a quoted `expectedInstructionsRevision` the project has moved past is refused `409 PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_DRIFT` with nothing written, and updates are serialized so the check and the write never interleave. Each changed text is logged in `project_instruction_changes` (teardown-exempt), credited to the user, an analysis, a chat or a saved lesson (`lesson`, the source thread as its chat); consecutive user edits of one field within `PROJECT_CHANGE_COALESCE_MS` (10 min) extend one row. Every create, update, rearm and delete, and every move of the autopilot breaker (`TaskSettleService`), publishes `projects_changed {projectId, textsOnly?}` (`ProjectEventBus`) to every client, paired devices included; `textsOnly` marks an update that named only the texts. Any text an analysis writes is refused control characters (the service) and invisible format characters (the tool's reader).
- **A project's own chat** (`ProjectChatService` in `v1/teams`, `POST|DELETE /v1/projects/:projectId/chat`): one ordinary chat per project, in its folder and under it (`Run.projectId`), marked `Run.editsProjectId` with no `editsTeamSlug` (`isProjectChat`), hidden from the sidebar like a builder chat (`RunDao.listChats`, found by `listEditingProject`), deleted with its project (on `projects_changed` and by a boot sweep). `ChatService` re-briefs it before each turn from the source the service installs (`useProjectChat`: `utils/project-chat-instructions.ts`, the project's blocks with their revision, its description, and for a team default the members' Model groups, the overlay's path and the overlay as it stands — never the team file's path, the team being read through `read_teams`) and registers a `ProjectChatBroker` sink per turn carrying the project and its default team (null for an agent, or a team the library lost) — never the model's. It holds the same team and project tools as any chat (user decision), its own project being what a call naming none means: `save_project_instructions` (`blocks` + `baseRevision`, `projectId` for another project, no item ids; `ProjectInstructionsChangeService.saveForChat`, a drift answered with the blocks as they stand, the card's change carrying no ids and `cardSeq: null`, logged with source `chat`), `propose_team` in the one form (`.claude/rules/daemon-host-tools.md`) and `read_teams` over the whole library, approved without a card in every mode (`isHostLibraryReadCall`, on a project chat's or an analysis's turn only). Never `save_team_change`, `backup_team` or the analysis tools.
- `Task.folder` null means INHERIT the project's folder, resolved at read sites (Run press, `TaskQueueService`). Never snapshot it at create. Folders are canonicalized and checked where set.
- The autopilot queue split lives in `v1/tasks` (`TaskQueueService`, `TaskQueueController` keeps the projects path/tag/operationId) because of the module cycle. While armed it refuses team-targeted cards.
- Module direction: `TasksModule` imports `ProjectsModule`; `ProjectsModule` provides `TaskDao` / `LabelInstructionDao` rather than importing back. No cascades: `ProjectsService.remove` deletes tasks itself.
- `Task.position` is allocated past the column's live max, never from a count; only `reorder` renumbers (conditional per-card UPDATE).
- Label instructions go into `Run.taskInstructions`, a column separate from `customInstructions` (forget must not purge them). They are re-resolved on resume.
- `Task.runId` and `Run.taskId` are written together or not at all; `abandon` deletes the run it created.
- **The daemon runs no git** (worktrees: root `AGENTS.md` → *Storage split*). `TaskRunsService` starts an ordinary chat, or a team run; a later press continues the card's own run when it is a settled run of the same agent or team (`resumableRun`). Two start guards: the synchronous `TasksService.claimStart` claim and `assertNotAlreadyRunning` (asks the run). The board's reconcile holds off a card while its claim is held and makes it once the claim is released.
- `TaskSettleService` observes only. A run completing moves nothing and writes no report; never automate that from the settle. The settle only maps `failed` → `failed` and `cancelled` → `todo`, and only for a card still `in_progress`.
- A card whose own run starts or settles without the card moving is announced as `task_changed` with no `reason` (`TasksService.announceRunChanged`, from `TaskSettleService`), since the queue's live count moved; only on the live path, never from a reconcile, which every board load and `task_changed` runs. A move or a `work-finished` announce stands for it.
- Board tools (`HOST_BOARD_TOOLS`) go to every endpoint holder; every write goes through `TasksService`. Run-owned fields are refused; there is no delete tool.
- `refuseUnattended`: a chat not in `auto` may not put a card in an armed intake, change any card on an armed board (except the working run moving its own card out of intake), set `approval: 'auto'`, or change an existing card's run configuration. Keyed on the column the card ends in. The executor auto-approves no host tool but the shared memory's and the knowledge store's (`.claude/rules/daemon-memory.md`, `.claude/rules/daemon-knowledge.md`).
- A working run may move its card only to `TASK_AGENT_OWN_STATUSES`.
- Report screenshots are copied onto the card; rewrite the image destination by position. Keep existing copies (20-file cap).
- A run ended by a spent usage limit (`AgentAdapter.failureFrom`, read off its newest `error` row) never counts toward the failure breaker. It holds an armed project until the limit reopens (`Project.autopilotLimitUntil`: the named reset plus `AUTOPILOT_LIMIT_RESET_GRACE_MS`, else `AUTOPILOT_LIMIT_FALLBACK_MS` from now). The moment passing ends the hold, and a completed run or a re-arm ends it early. A person's Run press is not held.
- A cancelled card is marked `Task.stoppedAt` and never auto-restarted (`TASK_STOPPED_BY_USER`); only a Run press that starts it clears the mark.
- A worktree the autopilot could not prepare is reported by main (`POST /v1/tasks/:taskId/prepare-failed`, git's line as `reason`) and marked `Task.prepareFailedAt` (only while the card is still in the intake column; announced as `task_changed`, since nothing else makes the board re-read the queue). While armed, `TaskQueueService` holds a mark younger than `PREPARE_FAILURE_RETRY_MS` (5 min) in `blocked` BEFORE the cap, so it never takes the slot of the card behind it; an older one is handed out again (retry), a repeat failure re-stamps it, and any successful start clears it.
- Every `blocked` entry carries a `kind` (where the answer lies: `run-target` / `missing-team` in the run settings, `stopped` / `worktree` by a Run press on the card, `overlay` on the team's page in the project's scope) and a `retryInMs` (set only for `worktree`: what is left of the hold, relative so a client on another clock times it right — nothing announces it lapsing, so the board re-reads the queue then). The worktree hold is asked last, so a card with a run-settings problem is reported as that.
- `work-finished` is announced only for a card in `done` with no run working and no live work outside a turn (`RunLiveWork` in `v1/agents`: a monitor, a command action, a detached command or background sub-agent, monitor output or a host wake waiting to start a turn; the team module adds its own through `register`) — each runs in, or wakes the agent into, the card's worktree. A card withheld for live work is asked again on every `RunLiveWork.changes()` and announced once free; the hold is in memory, since a restart ends monitors and command actions and main's reaper asks `POST /v1/tasks/finished` at every daemon start. `reviveFailedCard` lifts only `failed`. `run_deleted` clears the edge, keeps the report and never touches the worktree.
- Stop cause (`utils/stop-cause.ts`, twin of the UI's `writeStopCause`): `main.ts` reads and deletes it once, before both reconciles, then runs `settleCutOffCards(null)`. `crash` / `unknown` mark interrupted cards stopped (`TASK_INTERRUPTED`); `quit` / `self-update` / `restart` continue unattended.
- `create_task` defaults a new card to `backlog`; never default one into an intake column — an armed project starts an agent on it within seconds.
- A reported merge (`POST /v1/tasks/:taskId/pull-request-merged`) moves only a card still `in_review`, only for a URL its run captured (else `TASK_PULL_REQUEST_UNKNOWN`); a card moved elsewhere is a no-op.
- A task file is bound by PATH, never copied; `DELETE /v1/tasks/:taskId/files/:attachmentId` drops the reference only and never deletes the user's file.
